  <p><strong>Which sports-bra padding option should a brand specify?</strong> Start with the user problem, not the pad alone. A yoga bra designed for light coverage, a training bra that prioritizes stability and a lifestyle crop top may all need different cup and lining decisions. The band, straps, fabric, neckline and cup construction work together; no pad type by itself guarantees support.</p>

  <h2>Removable pads: flexibility with a pocket to engineer</h2>
  <p>Removable inserts allow the wearer to choose a padded or unpadded look and can make replacement possible. The buyer still needs to define the opening location, pocket depth, pad orientation and how the insert stays flat. During fitting, check whether the pad shifts during reaching, bending and repeated wear. After washing, inspect whether it folds, twists or becomes difficult to return to position. A product page should clearly state that the pads are removable if that is the approved construction.</p>

  <h2>Sewn-in pads: fewer loose parts, more placement decisions</h2>
  <p>A sewn-in pad avoids a separate component that the customer must reinsert. It also fixes the pad position, so the sample review should focus on stitching comfort, edge visibility, symmetry and fit across sizes. A pad that looks smooth on a flat garment may sit differently on the body. Ask for front, side and movement views during approval and check the result after care cycles.</p>

  <h2>Molded cups: shape needs size-specific approval</h2>
  <p>Molded cups create a more defined silhouette. The cup shape, spacing and depth must be judged across the intended size range; simply scaling one sample can leave gaping or compression. Confirm whether the cup is integral, inserted or attached to a separate lining, because those constructions have different feel and production steps. The commercial discussion should identify which cup sizes and forms are actually available before promising a full size range.</p>

  <h2>Do not overlook the unpadded option</h2>
  <p>Some customers prefer no foam at all. An unpadded sports bra can still use an appropriate lining and well-designed band, straps and fabric. Its suitability depends on intended coverage, opacity, activity and the brand's product promise. Compare it in the same light and movement conditions as the padded options instead of assuming one route is universally better.</p>

  <h2>Put these decisions in the sample brief</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Target activity and support intention; intended size range and fit model sizes.</li>
    <li>Padding route, shape, thickness direction and whether pads are removable.</li>
    <li>Outer fabric and lining references; desired coverage in relevant lighting.</li>
    <li>Pocket opening and retention details, or seam and attachment details.</li>
    <li>Front, side and back fit observations during movement.</li>
    <li>Care instructions to verify and a post-wash appearance check.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For laundering review, the <a href="https://www.aatcc.org/learn/online-test-method-training/laundering-module" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">AATCC laundering-method overview</a> identifies established methods for assessing garment dimensional and appearance changes. It does not prove that a particular FITCLO bra has passed a test; a project-specific test plan and results would need to be agreed separately.</p>

  <h2>How this topic fits the wider sports-bra decision</h2>
  <p>Our <a href="/blogs/sports-bra-sourcing-guide-support-level-band-straps-cups-size-grading/">sports-bra sourcing guide</a> covers band, straps, cup architecture and grading. Use this page when the padding choice itself is unsettled, then use the <a href="/blogs/sports-bra-quality-testing-support-recovery-strap-and-band-checks/">quality-testing guide</a> for the broader wear review. If you want to adapt an existing style, ask which pad treatments can be changed without redesigning the complete bra; a new cup architecture may require a development route rather than a simple private-label change.</p>
